# The three addresses a hospital's server answers (pilot step 26).
#
# Each origin in deploy/.env is a site here. Given as https://… (or a bare
# name) Caddy obtains and renews its certificate itself; given as http://… it
# is served plainly, for trying the stack on a machine with no public name.
#
# STAFF_NETWORKS (deploy/.env, DEPLOY.md S2) is where staff may sign in from.
# docker-compose.yml always passes a list: the hospital's addresses when they
# are set, everywhere (0.0.0.0/0 ::/0) when they are not. Outside it the staff
# console does not open and the API refuses every staff sign-in route, so
# nobody elsewhere can guess a password or lock an account. Patients' pages and
# the rest of the API are not affected.

{$APP_ORIGIN} {
	encode gzip
	reverse_proxy patient:3000
}

{$CONSOLE_ORIGIN} {
	encode gzip
	@outside_staff_networks not remote_ip {$STAFF_NETWORKS}
	handle @outside_staff_networks {
		header Content-Type "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
		respond "এই কনসোল শুধু হাসপাতালের নেটওয়ার্ক থেকে খোলে।
This console opens only from the hospital's network." 403
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy console:3100
	}
}

{$API_ORIGIN} {
	encode gzip
	@staff_signin_outside {
		path /api/v1/staff /api/v1/staff/*
		not remote_ip {$STAFF_NETWORKS}
	}
	handle @staff_signin_outside {
		header Content-Type "application/json; charset=utf-8"
		respond `{"ok":false,"error":{"code":"AUTH_FORBIDDEN_SCOPE","message":"Staff sign-in is open only from the hospital's network.","details":{"reason":"outside_staff_networks"}}}` 403
	}
	handle {
		# The realtime socket upgrades on this same address (BACKEND.md §6).
		reverse_proxy api:4000
	}
}

# A hospital's portal is the patient app at another name (FR-BRD-07, plan C2):
# <hospital code>.<PLATFORM_DOMAIN>, or a domain the hospital owns and a
# platform administrator has recorded. The app and the API already answer for
# those names; this file has to as well, and that part is yours, because it
# needs DNS and certificates the product cannot arrange:
#
#   - for every hospital under the platform's domain, a wildcard record and a
#     wildcard certificate (Caddy gets one through a DNS challenge, which needs
#     the build of Caddy with your DNS provider's module):
#
#       *.medlivebd.example {
#       	encode gzip
#       	reverse_proxy patient:3000
#       }
#
#   - for a hospital's own domain, once it points here, a site of its own,
#     for which Caddy obtains the certificate itself:
#
#       portal.example-hospital.com.bd {
#       	encode gzip
#       	reverse_proxy patient:3000
#       }
#
# Names under the platform's domain that are the platform's own (the console,
# the API) keep their own sites above, and a more specific site wins.
